=15$, $+11=26$, $+8=34$, $+5=39$, $+9=48$, $+11=59$. $59\\mod13=59-52=7$. ✓\n\nFor $n=11$: exponent $=121-4k-4=117-4k$. $117=9\\cdot12+9$, $117\\equiv9$. $2^{117-4k}\\equiv 2^9\\cdot 2^{-4k}\\equiv 5\\cdot 9^k$. ✓\n\n$5\\cdot9^k$: \n- $k=1$: $5\\cdot9=45\\equiv6$ ✓\n- $k=2$: $5\\cdot3=15\\equiv2$ ✓\n- $k=3$: $5\\cdot1=5$ ✓\n- $k=4$: $5\\cdot9=45\\equiv6$ ✓\n- $k=5$: $5\\cdot3=15\\equiv2$ ✓\n- $k=6$: $5\\cdot1=5$ ✓\n- $k=7$: $6$ ✓\n- $k=8$: $2$ ✓\n- $k=9$: $5$ ✓\n\n$k(10-k)$:\n- $k=1$: 9, $k=2$: 16, $k=3$: 21, $k=4$: 24, $k=5$: 25, $k=6$: 24, $k=7$: 21, $k=8$: 16, $k=9$: 9. ✓\n\nTerms:\n- $k=1$: $9\\cdot6=54\\equiv54-52=2$ ✓\n- $k=2$: $16\\cdot2=32\\equiv32-26=6$ ✓\n- $k=3$: $21\\cdot5=105$, $13\\cdot8=104$, $105-104=1$ ✓\n- $k=4$: $24\\cdot6=144$, $13\\cdot11=143$, $144-143=1$ ✓\n- $k=5$: $25\\cdot2=50\\equiv50-39=11$ ✓\n- $k=6$: $24\\cdot5=120\\equiv120-117=3$ ✓\n- $k=7$: $21\\cdot6=126\\equiv126-117=9$ ✓\n- $k=8$: $16\\cdot2=32\\equiv6$ ✓\n- $k=9$: $9\\cdot5=45\\equiv45-39=6$ ✓\n\nSum: $2+6+1+1+11+3+9+6+6$. $2+6=8,+1=9,+1=10,+11=21,+3=24,+9=33,+6=39,+6=45$. $45\\mod13=45-39=6$. ✓\n\nFor $n=12$: exponent $=144-4k-4=140-4k$. $140=11\\cdot12+8$, $140\\equiv8$. $2^8\\equiv9$. $2^{140-4k}\\equiv 9\\cdot 9^k=9^{k+1}$. ✓\n\n$9^{k+1}$ for $k=1..10$ ($k+1=2..11$): $9^2\\equiv3, 9^3\\equiv1, 9^4\\equiv9, 9^5\\equiv3, 9^6\\equiv1, 9^7\\equiv9, 9^8\\equiv3, 9^9\\equiv1, 9^{10}\\equiv9, 9^{11}\\equiv3$.\n\nSo: 3,1,9,3,1,9,3,1,9,3. ✓\n\n$k(11-k)$:\n- $k=1$: 10, $k=2$: 18, $k=3$: 24, $k=4$: 28, $k=5$: 30, $k=6$: 30, $k=7$: 28, $k=8$: 24, $k=9$: 18, $k=10$: 10. ✓\n\nTerms:\n- $k=1$: $10\\cdot3=30\\equiv4$ ✓\n- $k=2$: $18\\cdot1=18\\equiv5$ ✓\n- $k=3$: $24\\cdot9=216$, $13\\cdot16=208$, $216-208=8$ ✓\n- $k=4$: $28\\cdot3=84\\equiv84-78=6$ ✓\n- $k=5$: $30\\cdot1=30\\equiv4$ ✓\n- $k=6$: $30\\cdot9=270$, $13\\cdot20=260$, $270-260=10$ ✓\n- $k=7$: $28\\cdot3=84\\equiv6$ ✓\n- $k=8$: $24\\cdot1=24\\equiv11$ ✓\n- $k=9$: $18\\cdot9=162$, $13\\cdot12=156$, $162-156=6$ ✓\n- $k=10$: $10\\cdot3=30\\equiv4$ ✓\n\nSum: $4+5+8+6+4+10+6+11+6+4$. $4+5=9,+8=17,+6=23,+4=27,+10=37,+6=43,+11=54,+6=60,+4=64$. $64\\mod13=64-52=12$. ✓\n\nTotal: $7+6+12=25\\equiv12\\pmod{13}$.\n\nSo the validation key is $\\boxed{12}$.\n\nLet me also sanity-check the closed form with one more value, say $f(4)$.\n\n$f(4)$ from recurrence ($n=2$): $f(4) = 4^4 f(3) - 16^3 f(2) + 2\\cdot 2^4 = 256\\cdot 2 - 4096\\cdot 0 + 2\\cdot 16 = 512 + 32 = 544$.\n\nFrom formula: $f(4) = \\sum_{k=1}^{2} k(3-k) 2^{16-4k-4} = \\sum_{k=1}^{2} k(3-k)2^{12-4k}$.\n- $k=1$: $1\\cdot2\\cdot 2^8 = 2\\cdot256=512$\n- $k=2$: $2\\cdot1\\cdot 2^4 = 2\\cdot16=32$\n- Total: $544$. ✓\n\nLet me also verify $f(4)\\pmod{13}$: $544 = 13\\cdot41 + 1 = 533+11=544$, so $544\\equiv 544-13\\cdot41=544-533=11\\pmod{13}$.\n\nUsing our method for $n=4$: exponent $=16-4k-4=12-4k$. $12\\equiv0\\pmod{12}$, so $2^{12-4k}\\equiv 2^{-4k}\\equiv 9^k$.\n\n$k(3-k)$: $k=1$: 2, $k=2$: 2.\n- $k=1$: $2\\cdot9=18\\equiv5$\n- $k=2$: $2\\cdot3=6$\nSum: $5+6=11$. ✓ Matches $544\\equiv11$.\n\nGreat, everything checks out.\n\nThe answer is $12$.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1786762460}
{"type": "step", "node_id": 24, "parent_node_id": 22, "role": "assistant", "content": "## Solution\n\n**Recurrence.** We have $f(0)=f(1)=0$ and\n$$f(n+2)=4^{n+2}f(n+1)-16^{n+1}f(n)+n\\cdot 2^{n^2}.$$\n\n**Substitution.** Let $f(n)=2^{n^2}h(n)$. Then $f(n+2)=2^{(n+2)^2}h(n+2)$, and the recurrence becomes (after dividing by $2^{n^2}$):\n$$2^{4n+4}h(n+2)=2^{4n+5}h(n+1)-2^{4n+4}h(n)+n,$$\ni.e.\n$$h(n+2)-2h(n+1)+h(n)=\\frac{n}{16^{n+1}}.$$\n\nThe homogeneous solutions are $A+Bn$. From $f(0)=f(1)=0$ we get $h(0)=h(1)=0$, so $A=B=0$. Using the discrete Green's function for $\\Delta^2 h(n)=g(n)$ with $h(0)=h(1)=0$:\n$$h(n)=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}(n-1-k)\\,g(k)=\\sum_{k=1}^{n-2}(n-1-k)\\frac{k}{16^{k+1}}.$$\n\nTherefore\n$$f(n)=2^{n^2}\\sum_{k=1}^{n-2}\\frac{k(n-1-k)}{16^{k+1}}=\\sum_{k=1}^{n-2}k(n-1-k)\\,2^{n^2-4k-4}.$$\n\n**Verification.** $f(4)=1\\cdot2\\cdot2^8+2\\cdot1\\cdot2^4=512+32=544$, matching the recurrence: $f(4)=256f(3)-4096f(2)+2\\cdot16=256\\cdot2+32=544$. ✓\n\n**Modular computation.** Since $2^{12}\\equiv1\\pmod{13}$ and $2^4\\equiv3$, we have $2^{-4}\\equiv3^{-1}\\equiv9\\pmod{13}$, with $9^k$ cycling $(9,3,1)$ of period 3.\n\n**$f(10)$:** exponent $96-4k\\equiv -4k$, so $2^{96-4k}\\equiv9^k$. With $k(9-k)$ for $k=1,\\dots,8$:\n\n| $k$ | $k(9-k)$ | $9^k$ | product mod 13 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n|1|8|9|7|\n|2|14|3|3|\n|3|18|1|5|\n|4|20|9|11|\n|5|20|3|8|\n|6|18|1|5|\n|7|14|9|9|\n|8|8|3|11|\n\nSum $=59\\equiv7\\pmod{13}$.\n\n**$f(11)$:** exponent $117-4k\\equiv9-4k$, so $2^{117-4k}\\equiv2^9\\cdot9^k\\equiv5\\cdot9^k$. With $k(10-k)$ for $k=1,\\dots,9$:\n\n| $k$ | $k(10-k)$ | $5\\cdot9^k$ | product mod 13 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n|1|9|6|2|\n|2|16|2|6|\n|3|21|5|1|\n|4|24|6|1|\n|5|25|2|11|\n|6|24|5|3|\n|7|21|6|9|\n|8|16|2|6|\n|9|9|5|6|\n\nSum $=45\\equiv6\\pmod{13}$.\n\n**$f(12)$:** exponent $140-4k\\equiv8-4k$, so $2^{140-4k}\\equiv2^8\\cdot9^k\\equiv9^{k+1}$. With $k(11-k)$ for $k=1,\\dots,10$:\n\n| $k$ | $k(11-k)$ | $9^{k+1}$ | product mod 13 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n|1|10|3|4|\n|2|18|1|5|\n|3|24|9|8|\n|4|28|3|6|\n|5|30|1|4|\n|6|30|9|10|\n|7|28|3|6|\n|8|24|1|11|\n|9|18|9|6|\n|10|10|3|4|\n\nSum $=64\\equiv12\\pmod{13}$.\n\n**Final key:**\n$$f(10)+f(11)+f(12)\\equiv7+6+12=25\\equiv\\boxed{12}\\pmod{13}.$$\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me parse the recurrence carefully.\n\nWe have $f(0) = 0$, $f(1) = 0$.\n\nFor $n = 0, 1, 2, \\dots$:\n$$f(n+2) = 4^{n+2} \\cdot f(n+1) - 16^{n+1} \\cdot f(n) + n \\cdot 2^{n^2}$$\n\nLet me verify the entropy term: \"an external entropy value equal to $n \\cdot 2^{n^2}$ is added.\"\n\nSo $f(n+2) = 4^{n+2} f(n+1) - 16^{n+1} f(n) + n \\cdot 2^{n^2}$.\n\nLet me try to find a closed form. Note $4^{n+2} = 2^{2(n+2)} = 2^{2n+4}$ and $16^{n+1} = 2^{4(n+1)} = 2^{4n+4}$.\n\nLet me try substitution $f(n) = 2^{n^2} g(n)$ or similar. Actually let me think about the homogeneous part.\n\nHomogeneous: $f(n+2) = 4^{n+2} f(n+1) - 16^{n+1} f(n)$.\n\nLet me try $f(n) = 2^{an^2 + bn + c}$. Then:\n- $f(n+2) = 2^{a(n+2)^2 + b(n+2) + c}$\n- $f(n+1) = 2^{a(n+1)^2 + b(n+1) + c}$\n- $f(n) = 2^{an^2 + bn + c}$\n\nHomogeneous equation: $2^{a(n+2)^2+b(n+2)+c} = 2^{2n+4} \\cdot 2^{a(n+1)^2+b(n+1)+c} - 2^{4n+4}\\cdot 2^{an^2+bn+c}$.\n\nThis is a difference of two terms, not easy to match with single exponential. Let me try $f(n) = 2^{n^2} h(n)$.\n\nThen $f(n+2) = 2^{(n+2)^2} h(n+2)$, $f(n+1) = 2^{(n+1)^2} h(n+1)$, $f(n) = 2^{n^2} h(n)$.\n\nRHS: $4^{n+2} \\cdot 2^{(n+1)^2} h(n+1) - 16^{n+1}\\cdot 2^{n^2} h(n) + n\\cdot 2^{n^2}$.\n\n$4^{n+2} = 2^{2n+4}$, so first term: $2^{2n+4+(n+1)^2} h(n+1) = 2^{2n+4+n^2+2n+1} h(n+1) = 2^{n^2+4n+5} h(n+1)$.\n\n$16^{n+1} = 2^{4n+4}$, so second term: $2^{4n+4+n^2} h(n)$.\n\nLHS: $2^{(n+2)^2} h(n+2) = 2^{n^2+4n+4} h(n+2)$.\n\nSo dividing everything by $2^{n^2}$:\n\n$2^{4n+4} h(n+2) = 2^{4n+5} h(n+1) - 2^{4n+4} h(n) + n$.\n\nDivide by $2^{4n+4}$:\n\n$h(n+2) = 2 h(n+1) - h(n) + \\frac{n}{2^{4n+4}}$.\n\nSo $h(n+2) - 2h(n+1) + h(n) = \\frac{n}{2^{4n+4}} = \\frac{n}{16^{n+1}}$.\n\nThe homogeneous part $h(n+2)-2h(n+1)+h(n)=0$ has solutions $h(n) = A + Bn$ (double root 1).\n\nSo $f(n) = 2^{n^2}(A + Bn) + $ particular solution.\n\nInitial conditions: $f(0) = 0 \\Rightarrow 2^0(A + 0) = A = 0$. So $A = 0$.\n$f(1) = 0 \\Rightarrow 2^1(B\\cdot 1) = 2B = 0 \\Rightarrow B = 0$.\n\nSo the homogeneous part is zero, and $f(n) = 2^{n^2} h(n)$ where $h$ is the particular solution with $h(0)=h(1)=0$ (since $f(0)=f(1)=0$ and $2^{n^2}\\neq 0$).\n\nWait, but $h(0) = f(0)/2^0 = 0$, $h(1) = f(1)/2^1 = 0$. Yes.\n\nSo we need to solve:\n$$h(n+2) - 2h(n+1) + h(n) = \\frac{n}{16^{n+1}}, \\quad h(0)=h(1)=0.$$\n\nLet me denote $\\Delta^2 h(n) = h(n+2)-2h(n+1)+h(n) = \\frac{n}{16^{n+1}}$.\n\nThis is a second-order difference (discrete second derivative). We can solve by summation.\n\nRecall: if $\\Delta^2 h(n) = g(n)$, then $h(n) = h(0) + n\\,\\Delta h(0) + \\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}(n-1-k) g(k)$.\n\nActually, the Green's function: $h(n) = h(0) + n(h(1)-h(0)) + \\sum_{k=0}^{n-2} (n-1-k) g(k)$ where $g(k) = \\Delta^2 h(k)$.\n\nWith $h(0)=0, h(1)=0$: $h(n) = \\sum_{k=0}^{n-2} (n-1-k) \\frac{k}{16^{k+1}}$.\n\nSo $f(n) = 2^{n^2} \\sum_{k=0}^{n-2} (n-1-k) \\frac{k}{16^{k+1}}$.\n\nLet me verify for small $n$. For $n=2$: $f(2) = 2^4 \\sum_{k=0}^{0}(1-k)\\frac{k}{16^{k+1}} = 16 \\cdot (1-0)\\cdot 0 = 0$.\n\nCheck with recurrence: $f(2) = 4^2 f(1) - 16^1 f(0) + 0\\cdot 2^0 = 0 - 0 + 0 = 0$. ✓\n\nFor $n=3$: $f(3) = 2^9 \\sum_{k=0}^{1}(2-k)\\frac{k}{16^{k+1}} = 512\\left[(2-0)\\frac{0}{16} + (2-1)\\frac{1}{16^2}\\right] = 512 \\cdot \\frac{1}{256} = 2$.\n\nCheck with recurrence ($n=1$): $f(3) = 4^3 f(2) - 16^2 f(1) + 1\\cdot 2^1 = 64\\cdot 0 - 256\\cdot 0 + 2 = 2$. ✓\n\nGreat, the formula works.\n\nNow we need $f(10) + f(11) + f(12) \\pmod{13}$.\n\n$$f(n) = 2^{n^2} \\sum_{k=0}^{n-2} (n-1-k) \\frac{k}{16^{k+1}} = 2^{n^2} \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} (n-1-k) \\frac{k}{16^{k+1}}$$\n\n(k=0 term is 0).\n\n$= 2^{n^2} \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} \\frac{k(n-1-k)}{16^{k+1}} = 2^{n^2} \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} \\frac{k(n-1-k)}{2^{4k+4}}$.\n\n$= \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} \\frac{k(n-1-k) \\cdot 2^{n^2}}{2^{4k+4}} = \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} k(n-1-k) 2^{n^2 - 4k - 4}$.\n\nSo $f(n) = \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} k(n-1-k) \\cdot 2^{n^2-4k-4}$.\n\nNow I need to compute this mod 13 for $n = 10, 11, 12$.\n\nLet me compute $2^m \\pmod{13}$. The order of 2 mod 13: $2^1=2, 2^2=4, 2^3=8, 2^4=16\\equiv 3, 2^5=6, 2^6=12\\equiv -1, 2^{12}\\equiv 1$. So order 12.\n\n$2^m \\pmod{13}$ depends on $m \\pmod{12}$.\n\nLet me compute for each $n$ and each $k$.\n\n**$n=10$:** $k$ from 1 to 8. $n-1-k = 9-k$. $n^2 = 100$. Exponent: $100 - 4k - 4 = 96 - 4k$.\n\n$f(10) = \\sum_{k=1}^{8} k(9-k) 2^{96-4k}$.\n\n$96 - 4k \\pmod{12}$: $96 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}$, so $96 - 4k \\equiv -4k \\equiv 8k \\pmod{12}$ (since $-4 \\equiv 8$).\n\nActually $-4k \\pmod{12}$. Let me just compute each term.\n\n$2^{96-4k} \\pmod{13}$: Since $96 \\equiv 0\\pmod{12}$, $2^{96-4k} \\equiv 2^{-4k} \\equiv 2^{12-4k\\bmod 12}$... let me just compute $(-4k) \\bmod 12$ for each $k$.\n\nActually $2^{96-4k} = 2^{96}\\cdot 2^{-4k}$. $2^{96} = (2^{12})^8 \\equiv 1$. So $2^{96-4k}\\equiv 2^{-4k}\\pmod{13}$.\n\n$2^{-4k} = (2^{-4})^k$. $2^4 = 16\\equiv 3\\pmod{13}$. $3^{-1}\\pmod{13}$: $3\\cdot 9 = 27\\equiv 1$, so $3^{-1}\\equiv 9$. So $2^{-4}\\equiv 9\\pmod{13}$.\n\nSo $2^{96-4k}\\equiv 9^k \\pmod{13}$.\n\n$9^k \\pmod{13}$: $9^1=9, 9^2=81\\equiv 81-78=3, 9^3=27\\equiv 1, 9^4=9, ...$. So period 3: $9, 3, 1, 9, 3, 1, ...$.\n\n$k$: 1→9, 2→3, 3→1, 4→9, 5→3, 6→1, 7→9, 8→3.\n\nNow $k(9-k)$ for $k=1..8$:\n- $k=1$: $1\\cdot 8 = 8$\n- $k=2$: $2\\cdot 7 = 14$\n- $k=3$: $3\\cdot 6 = 18$\n- $k=4$: $4\\cdot 5 = 20$\n- $k=5$: $5\\cdot 4 = 20$\n- $k=6$: $6\\cdot 3 = 18$\n- $k=7$: $7\\cdot 2 = 14$\n- $k=8$: $8\\cdot 1 = 8$\n\nTerms mod 13:\n- $k=1$: $8\\cdot 9 = 72 \\equiv 72-65=7$\n- $k=2$: $14\\cdot 3 = 42 \\equiv 42-39=3$\n- $k=3$: $18\\cdot 1 = 18 \\equiv 5$\n- $k=4$: $20\\cdot 9 = 180 \\equiv 180-169=11$ (since $13\\cdot13=169$)\n- $k=5$: $20\\cdot 3 = 60 \\equiv 60-52=8$\n- $k=6$: $18\\cdot 1 = 18 \\equiv 5$\n- $k=7$: $14\\cdot 9 = 126 \\equiv 126-117=9$\n- $k=8$: $8\\cdot 3 = 24 \\equiv 11$\n\nSum: $7+3+5+11+8+5+9+11 = 59$. $59 \\pmod{13} = 59-52 = 7$.\n\nSo $f(10) \\equiv 7 \\pmod{13}$.\n\n**$n=11$:** $k$ from 1 to 9. $n-1-k = 10-k$. $n^2=121$. Exponent: $121-4k-4 = 117-4k$.\n\n$117 \\pmod{12}$: $117 = 9\\cdot12 + 9$, so $117\\equiv 9\\pmod{12}$. So $117-4k \\equiv 9-4k\\pmod{12}$.\n\n$2^{117-4k}\\equiv 2^{9-4k}\\pmod{13}$.\n\nLet me compute $2^{9-4k}\\pmod{13}$ for $k=1..9$. $2^9 = 512\\equiv 512-39\\cdot13=512-507=5$. So $2^9\\equiv 5$.\n\n$2^{9-4k} = 2^9 \\cdot 2^{-4k} \\equiv 5\\cdot 9^k \\pmod{13}$.\n\n$9^k$ for $k=1..9$: 9, 3, 1, 9, 3, 1, 9, 3, 1.\n\nSo $5\\cdot 9^k$: 45→$45-39=6$, 15→2, 5, 45→6, 15→2, 5, 45→6, 15→2, 5.\n\n$k(10-k)$ for $k=1..9$:\n- $k=1$: $1\\cdot9=9$\n- $k=2$: $2\\cdot8=16$\n- $k=3$: $3\\cdot7=21$\n- $k=4$: $4\\cdot6=24$\n- $k=5$: $5\\cdot5=25$\n- $k=6$: $6\\cdot4=24$\n- $k=7$: $7\\cdot3=21$\n- $k=8$: $8\\cdot2=16$\n- $k=9$: $9\\cdot1=9$\n\nTerms mod 13:\n- $k=1$: $9\\cdot6=54\\equiv54-52=2$\n- $k=2$: $16\\cdot2=32\\equiv32-26=6$\n- $k=3$: $21\\cdot5=105\\equiv105-91=14\\equiv1$ (13*8=104, 105-104=1)\n- $k=4$: $24\\cdot6=144\\equiv144-130=14\\equiv1$ (13*11=143, 144-143=1)\n- $k=5$: $25\\cdot2=50\\equiv50-39=11$\n- $k=6$: $24\\cdot5=120\\equiv120-117=3$\n- $k=7$: $21\\cdot6=126\\equiv126-117=9$\n- $k=8$: $16\\cdot2=32\\equiv6$\n- $k=9$: $9\\cdot5=45\\equiv6$\n\nSum: $2+6+1+1+11+3+9+6+6 = 45$. $45\\pmod{13}=45-39=6$.\n\nSo $f(11)\\equiv 6\\pmod{13}$.\n\n**$n=12$:** $k$ from 1 to 10. $n-1-k = 11-k$. $n^2=144$. Exponent: $144-4k-4=140-4k$.\n\n$140\\pmod{12}$: $140=11\\cdot12+8$, so $140\\equiv 8\\pmod{12}$. $140-4k\\equiv 8-4k\\pmod{12}$.\n\n$2^8 = 256\\equiv 256-19\\cdot13=256-247=9$. So $2^{140-4k}\\equiv 2^8\\cdot 2^{-4k}\\equiv 9\\cdot 9^k = 9^{k+1}\\pmod{13}$.\n\n$9^{k+1}$ for $k=1..10$: $k+1=2..11$, so $9^2,9^3,...,9^{11}$: 3,1,9,3,1,9,3,1,9,3.\n\n$k(11-k)$ for $k=1..10$:\n- $k=1$: $1\\cdot10=10$\n- $k=2$: $2\\cdot9=18$\n- $k=3$: $3\\cdot8=24$\n- $k=4$: $4\\cdot7=28$\n- $k=5$: $5\\cdot6=30$\n- $k=6$: $6\\cdot5=30$\n- $k=7$: $7\\cdot4=28$\n- $k=8$: $8\\cdot3=24$\n- $k=9$: $9\\cdot2=18$\n- $k=10$: $10\\cdot1=10$\n\nTerms mod 13:\n- $k=1$: $10\\cdot3=30\\equiv4$\n- $k=2$: $18\\cdot1=18\\equiv5$\n- $k=3$: $24\\cdot9=216\\equiv216-208=8$ (13*16=208)\n- $k=4$: $28\\cdot3=84\\equiv84-78=6$\n- $k=5$: $30\\cdot1=30\\equiv4$\n- $k=6$: $30\\cdot9=270\\equiv270-260=10$ (13*20=260)\n- $k=7$: $28\\cdot3=84\\equiv6$\n- $k=8$: $24\\cdot1=24\\equiv11$\n- $k=9$: $18\\cdot9=162\\equiv162-156=6$ (13*12=156)\n- $k=10$: $10\\cdot3=30\\equiv4$\n\nSum: $4+5+8+6+4+10+6+11+6+4 = 64$. $64\\pmod{13}=64-52=12\\equiv -1$.\n\nSo $f(12)\\equiv 12\\pmod{13}$.\n\n**Total:** $f(10)+f(11)+f(12) \\equiv 7+6+12 = 25 \\equiv 25-13=12\\pmod{13}$.\n\nWait, let me double check. $7+6+12=25$, $25\\pmod{13}=12$.\n\nHmm, let me re-verify some of the computations carefully.\n\nLet me re-verify $f(10)$.\n\nActually, let me re-derive the exponent reduction more carefully.\n\n$f(n) = \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} k(n-1-k) 2^{n^2-4k-4}$.\n\nFor $n=10$: exponent $= 100-4k-4 = 96-4k$.\n\n$2^{96-4k}\\pmod{13}$. $96 = 8\\cdot12$, so $2^{96}\\equiv 1$. $2^{-4k} = (2^4)^{-k} = 16^{-k}\\equiv 3^{-k}\\pmod{13}$. $3^{-1}\\equiv 9$, so $3^{-k}\\equiv 9^k$. ✓\n\n$9^k$ period 3: $9,3,1,...$. ✓\n\nLet me recompute terms:\n- $k=1$: $k(9-k)=1\\cdot8=8$, $9^1=9$, $8\\cdot9=72$, $72\\mod13$: $13\\cdot5=65$, $72-65=7$. ✓\n- $k=2$: $2\\cdot7=14$, $9^2=81\\equiv3$, $14\\cdot3=42$, $42-39=3$. ✓\n- $k=3$: $3\\cdot6=18$, $9^3\\equiv1$, $18\\cdot1=18$, $18-13=5$. ✓\n- $k=4$: $4\\cdot5=20$, $9^4\\equiv9$, $20\\cdot9=180$, $13\\cdot13=169$, $180-169=11$. ✓\n- $k=5$: $5\\cdot4=20$, $9^5\\equiv3$, $20\\cdot3=60$, $60-52=8$. ✓\n- $k=6$: $6\\cdot3=18$, $9^6\\equiv1$, $18\\cdot1=18\\equiv5$. ✓\n- $k=7$: $7\\cdot2=14$, $9^7\\equiv9$, $14\\cdot9=126$, $13\\cdot9=117$, $126-117=9$. ✓\n- $k=8$: $8\\cdot1=8$, $9^8\\equiv3$, $8\\cdot3=24$, $24-13=11$. ✓\n\nSum: $7+3+5+11+8+5+9+11$. $7+3=10$, $+5=15$, $+11=26$, $+8=34$, $+5=39$, $+9=48$, $+11=59$. $59\\mod13=59-52=7$. ✓\n\nFor $n=11$: exponent $=121-4k-4=117-4k$. $117=9\\cdot12+9$, $117\\equiv9$. $2^{117-4k}\\equiv 2^9\\cdot 2^{-4k}\\equiv 5\\cdot 9^k$. ✓\n\n$5\\cdot9^k$: \n- $k=1$: $5\\cdot9=45\\equiv6$ ✓\n- $k=2$: $5\\cdot3=15\\equiv2$ ✓\n- $k=3$: $5\\cdot1=5$ ✓\n- $k=4$: $5\\cdot9=45\\equiv6$ ✓\n- $k=5$: $5\\cdot3=15\\equiv2$ ✓\n- $k=6$: $5\\cdot1=5$ ✓\n- $k=7$: $6$ ✓\n- $k=8$: $2$ ✓\n- $k=9$: $5$ ✓\n\n$k(10-k)$:\n- $k=1$: 9, $k=2$: 16, $k=3$: 21, $k=4$: 24, $k=5$: 25, $k=6$: 24, $k=7$: 21, $k=8$: 16, $k=9$: 9. ✓\n\nTerms:\n- $k=1$: $9\\cdot6=54\\equiv54-52=2$ ✓\n- $k=2$: $16\\cdot2=32\\equiv32-26=6$ ✓\n- $k=3$: $21\\cdot5=105$, $13\\cdot8=104$, $105-104=1$ ✓\n- $k=4$: $24\\cdot6=144$, $13\\cdot11=143$, $144-143=1$ ✓\n- $k=5$: $25\\cdot2=50\\equiv50-39=11$ ✓\n- $k=6$: $24\\cdot5=120\\equiv120-117=3$ ✓\n- $k=7$: $21\\cdot6=126\\equiv126-117=9$ ✓\n- $k=8$: $16\\cdot2=32\\equiv6$ ✓\n- $k=9$: $9\\cdot5=45\\equiv45-39=6$ ✓\n\nSum: $2+6+1+1+11+3+9+6+6$. $2+6=8,+1=9,+1=10,+11=21,+3=24,+9=33,+6=39,+6=45$. $45\\mod13=45-39=6$. ✓\n\nFor $n=12$: exponent $=144-4k-4=140-4k$. $140=11\\cdot12+8$, $140\\equiv8$. $2^8\\equiv9$. $2^{140-4k}\\equiv 9\\cdot 9^k=9^{k+1}$. ✓\n\n$9^{k+1}$ for $k=1..10$ ($k+1=2..11$): $9^2\\equiv3, 9^3\\equiv1, 9^4\\equiv9, 9^5\\equiv3, 9^6\\equiv1, 9^7\\equiv9, 9^8\\equiv3, 9^9\\equiv1, 9^{10}\\equiv9, 9^{11}\\equiv3$.\n\nSo: 3,1,9,3,1,9,3,1,9,3. ✓\n\n$k(11-k)$:\n- $k=1$: 10, $k=2$: 18, $k=3$: 24, $k=4$: 28, $k=5$: 30, $k=6$: 30, $k=7$: 28, $k=8$: 24, $k=9$: 18, $k=10$: 10. ✓\n\nTerms:\n- $k=1$: $10\\cdot3=30\\equiv4$ ✓\n- $k=2$: $18\\cdot1=18\\equiv5$ ✓\n- $k=3$: $24\\cdot9=216$, $13\\cdot16=208$, $216-208=8$ ✓\n- $k=4$: $28\\cdot3=84\\equiv84-78=6$ ✓\n- $k=5$: $30\\cdot1=30\\equiv4$ ✓\n- $k=6$: $30\\cdot9=270$, $13\\cdot20=260$, $270-260=10$ ✓\n- $k=7$: $28\\cdot3=84\\equiv6$ ✓\n- $k=8$: $24\\cdot1=24\\equiv11$ ✓\n- $k=9$: $18\\cdot9=162$, $13\\cdot12=156$, $162-156=6$ ✓\n- $k=10$: $10\\cdot3=30\\equiv4$ ✓\n\nSum: $4+5+8+6+4+10+6+11+6+4$. $4+5=9,+8=17,+6=23,+4=27,+10=37,+6=43,+11=54,+6=60,+4=64$. $64\\mod13=64-52=12$. ✓\n\nTotal: $7+6+12=25\\equiv12\\pmod{13}$.\n\nSo the validation key is $\\boxed{12}$.\n\nLet me also sanity-check the closed form with one more value, say $f(4)$.\n\n$f(4)$ from recurrence ($n=2$): $f(4) = 4^4 f(3) - 16^3 f(2) + 2\\cdot 2^4 = 256\\cdot 2 - 4096\\cdot 0 + 2\\cdot 16 = 512 + 32 = 544$.\n\nFrom formula: $f(4) = \\sum_{k=1}^{2} k(3-k) 2^{16-4k-4} = \\sum_{k=1}^{2} k(3-k)2^{12-4k}$.\n- $k=1$: $1\\cdot2\\cdot 2^8 = 2\\cdot256=512$\n- $k=2$: $2\\cdot1\\cdot 2^4 = 2\\cdot16=32$\n- Total: $544$. ✓\n\nLet me also verify $f(4)\\pmod{13}$: $544 = 13\\cdot41 + 1 = 533+11=544$, so $544\\equiv 544-13\\cdot41=544-533=11\\pmod{13}$.\n\nUsing our method for $n=4$: exponent $=16-4k-4=12-4k$. $12\\equiv0\\pmod{12}$, so $2^{12-4k}\\equiv 2^{-4k}\\equiv 9^k$.\n\n$k(3-k)$: $k=1$: 2, $k=2$: 2.\n- $k=1$: $2\\cdot9=18\\equiv5$\n- $k=2$: $2\\cdot3=6$\nSum: $5+6=11$. ✓ Matches $544\\equiv11$.\n\nGreat, everything checks out.\n\nThe answer is $12$.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1786762460}
